  "query_preview": "..\n\nMostly clear skies across southern Arizona today with high\nclouds moving in from the west. Those high clouds will continue\nto push eastward into the area becoming partly to mostly cloudy\nby the evening hours. The upper level pattern today is driven by a\nhigh amplitude ridge that will quickly be eroded away by an \nincoming trough from the west coast. A cut off low will extend \noff of the main trough and sweep into northern Sonora, MX \novernight. The low will move eastward quickly, followed by a \nridge building in from the west. The ridge will raise daytime \nhigh temperatures to 3 to 9 degrees above normal by Friday, with\nhighs forecast to be in the upper 70s to low 80s. Dry \nconditions and above normal temperatures will persist as ridging\ncontinues through early next week. \n\nLooking ahead in the extended forecast, a pattern change at the\nend of next week could bring back chances for precipitation and\ncooler temperatures. The current Climate Prediction Center 6 to\n10 day outlook has a 40 to 60% chance for above normal\nprecipitation (normal for this time of year is 0.03 inches).\nConfidence in track, timing, and intensity of this change will\nimprove in the next several days.",
